A||||8||STUK'N'80s||85 GT & it's dim dash lights...WWHHYYY??||||9||
Z||000000||STUK'N'80s||04-25-2001||12:46 AM||||Can anyone explain to me why the dash lights on 85 GT's are so dim?? even when the brightness dial is on max. Is there a way I can significantly increase the brightness level, short of getting a new cluster etc. of a 86,87, or 88 dash setup?||||reg||9
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-25-2001||01:16 AM||||Funny you should mention that today. I was going over my 85 GT and that has always bugged me! I was thinking that my alternator might be running low but when I hooked it up while it was running I was getting an okay charge. Is this a problem for other years?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||04-25-2001||01:32 AM||||I may be worng but... I heard that the dash lights start to dim because the dimmer slider (under the headlight switch) may be going bad. I changed mine & it cleared the problem right up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">    </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||10
Z||000003||Monkeyman||04-25-2001||06:32 AM||||I think you'll find that most of the reflector gauges are pretty dim.  Find someone with some later model backlit gauges and you'll see what I mean.  <P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-25-2001||01:40 PM||||Im game to replace the dimmer! What could it hurt?||||reg||7
Z||000005||mrfixit58||04-25-2001||02:54 PM||||If I'm not mistaken, the dimmer feeds a power transistor behind the dash. The dimmer is just a variable carbon track resistor that goes from 0 to 230 ohms. If you ave a multimeter, I believe it's the center two posts on the plug. If the dimmer checks OK, it could be the power transistor.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektyr||04-25-2001||03:35 PM||||Keep one thing in mind here... bright dash means bad night vision.<P>It could be argued that the headlights of the other cars do more damage to your nightvision than the dash, but if I'm out on a highway at night by myself, I dial the dash lights way back and let my eyes adjust to the dark.  I usually set my dash lights to the dimmest position I can still read them at.  I figure ever little bit helps, especially when I'm behind the wheel of a car that must be <B>driven</B>, not simply manned.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||04-26-2001||10:09 PM||||The light must reflect off the back of the gage and the paint has lost it's reflectivity...<BR>Repaint with bright aluminum paint for white lighting, or tint over the aluminum with red dayglow for red lighting, or try green dayglow for green!!!<BR>BUT BE SURE TO BASE COAT WITH BRIGHT ALUMINUM!<BR>This will give better reflectivity than white...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000008||fyerstarter||04-26-2001||10:47 PM||||what is this day glow you speak of?||||reg||1
